1
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,160
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:38,303
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:15,675 --> 00:01:18,143
♪ ♪

5
00:02:21,642 --> 00:02:24,576
♪ ♪

6
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:40,002
We'll make time stand still.

7
00:02:43,364 --> 00:02:44,930
I wish I could.

8
00:02:47,634 --> 00:02:49,835
It's past 5:00.

9
00:02:51,304 --> 00:02:55,674
Oh, no, no, Michael's
never home before 7:00.

10
00:02:55,742 --> 00:02:57,587
Now, what should we
do to pass the time?

11
00:02:57,611 --> 00:03:00,244
Hmm.

12
00:03:04,151 --> 00:03:06,651
Oh, my gosh.

13
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:17,395
Shelby.

14
00:03:17,464 --> 00:03:19,224
It's Michael.

15
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:32,143
Shelby?

16
00:04:17,257 --> 00:04:20,325
Michael, you're home early.

17
00:04:29,436 --> 00:04:31,369
Ladies and gentlemen,

18
00:04:31,438 --> 00:04:34,339
may I introduce your
host for this evening?

19
00:04:34,408 --> 00:04:37,141
A man whose concern
for underprivileged children

20
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:40,412
worldwide has brought
this fundraiser about...

21
00:04:40,481 --> 00:04:43,214
Great Britain's ambassador
to the United Nations,

22
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:46,418
your friend, and my
colleague, Mr. Anthony Stewart.

23
00:04:59,366 --> 00:05:00,298
Thank you.

24
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:03,534
Thank you very much.

25
00:05:03,603 --> 00:05:05,837
Because of your generosity,

26
00:05:05,906 --> 00:05:10,575
children you'll never see
in countries you'll never visit

27
00:05:10,644 --> 00:05:12,288
are going to have
something to eat.

28
00:05:15,482 --> 00:05:19,317
It is you, ladies and gentlemen,
who deserve the applause.

29
00:05:19,386 --> 00:05:24,055
Thank you for
coming, and now enjoy.

30
00:05:36,402 --> 00:05:38,447
Good evening, Mrs. Russell.

31
00:05:38,471 --> 00:05:40,605
Hello, Mr. Stewart.

32
00:05:40,674 --> 00:05:43,842
I didn't want to
come here tonight.

33
00:05:43,910 --> 00:05:45,321
I left my watch in your bedroom.

34
00:05:45,345 --> 00:05:47,646
I know.

35
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:49,325
You brought it with
you? No, I couldn't.

36
00:05:49,349 --> 00:05:52,495
Michael... MICHAEL:
10:00 and all's well!

37
00:05:52,519 --> 00:05:56,621
That's what you Brits say
back in jolly old England, isn't it?

38
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:58,490
Only when we're quite drunk.

39
00:05:58,559 --> 00:06:01,826
Actually, I just wanted
to show off my new watch.

40
00:06:01,895 --> 00:06:03,028
See?

41
00:06:03,097 --> 00:06:07,198
I found it right next to
my bed, of all places.

42
00:06:07,267 --> 00:06:09,827
Would you like to hear
what's inscribed on the back?

43
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:10,870
Michael, please.

44
00:06:10,903 --> 00:06:12,870
I know what it says.

45
00:06:12,939 --> 00:06:14,005
Just give it to me.

46
00:06:14,073 --> 00:06:15,473
Give it to you?

47
00:06:50,043 --> 00:06:51,076
Hello.

48
00:06:51,144 --> 00:06:53,089
Anthony, you've
got to come over.

49
00:06:53,113 --> 00:06:54,479
Shelby?

50
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:56,659
Michael's still drinking,
and he's acting crazy.

51
00:06:56,683 --> 00:06:58,750
Please, come right
away, Anthony.

52
00:06:58,818 --> 00:07:00,051
I'm scared.

53
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:02,053
Please.

54
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:17,113
Good evening,
Mr. Stewart. Good evening.

55
00:07:29,015 --> 00:07:30,948
Shelby?

56
00:07:39,158 --> 00:07:41,092
Shelby?

57
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:48,500
Is anybody here?

58
00:08:46,793 --> 00:08:48,837
I've set up a barricade here.

59
00:08:51,097 --> 00:08:52,809
Up and down here, people.

60
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:54,113
Stay back.

61
00:09:00,907 --> 00:09:03,152
Hey, John, get me a
cup of coffee, will you?

62
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:18,423
Hello?

63
00:09:18,492 --> 00:09:19,824
Uh, I'm Alexander Winthrop.

64
00:09:19,893 --> 00:09:23,294
I'm an attorney with Benson,
Reese, Klein & Dunhill.

65
00:09:23,363 --> 00:09:24,603
I understand Lieutenant Rosetti

66
00:09:24,631 --> 00:09:26,597
is questioning
one of our clients...

67
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:29,767
Uh, Mr. Anthony Stewart.

68
00:09:29,836 --> 00:09:31,769
This way, sir.

69
00:09:34,708 --> 00:09:36,886
Fleeing the scene when
those police cars arrived

70
00:09:36,910 --> 00:09:39,177
was terribly
foolish, I realize that.

71
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:40,623
The truth is, I panicked.

72
00:09:40,647 --> 00:09:42,981
Could be you were just at a loss

73
00:09:43,049 --> 00:09:44,927
to explain those
bruises on your face.

74
00:09:44,951 --> 00:09:47,396
I got them during the
struggle with the killer.

75
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:48,630
Not with Michael Russell.

76
00:09:48,654 --> 00:09:49,687
Excuse me, gentlemen.

77
00:09:49,756 --> 00:09:52,056
Uh, I'm Alexander Winthrop.

78
00:09:52,125 --> 00:09:54,592
I'm-I'm with
Benson, Reese, Kline

79
00:09:54,661 --> 00:09:56,806
and Dunhill. Uh, we represent

80
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:59,030
the British consulate
in legal matters.

81
00:10:02,835 --> 00:10:04,769
Sir, perhaps you
shouldn't say any more.

82
00:10:06,506 --> 00:10:08,940
The ambassador isn't under
arrest, is he, Lieutenant?

83
00:10:09,009 --> 00:10:10,474
No, he has diplomatic immunity.

84
00:10:10,543 --> 00:10:12,043
I don't need immunity.

85
00:10:12,112 --> 00:10:13,656
- I haven't done anything!
- We have

86
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:16,081
an eyewitness says you
committed first-degree murder.

87
00:10:16,149 --> 00:10:17,482
That's impossible.

88
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:19,117
Victim's wife, Shelby Russell.

89
00:10:19,185 --> 00:10:21,118
According to her
sworn statement,

90
00:10:21,188 --> 00:10:22,632
you came bursting
into the apartment

91
00:10:22,656 --> 00:10:24,255
demanding that gold watch

92
00:10:24,324 --> 00:10:25,757
of yours. When Mr. Russell

93
00:10:25,825 --> 00:10:27,792
refused to give it to
you, a fight ensued

94
00:10:27,861 --> 00:10:29,472
which ended when you
bludgeoned him to death

95
00:10:29,496 --> 00:10:31,374
with a silver
candlestick holder.

96
00:10:31,398 --> 00:10:32,398
She's lying.

97
00:10:32,432 --> 00:10:33,465
She was not there.

98
00:10:33,533 --> 00:10:35,111
There was no one
in that apartment!

99
00:10:35,135 --> 00:10:37,079
You just said
somebody attacked you.

100
00:10:37,103 --> 00:10:38,636
Well, somebody<i> did</i> attack me!

101
00:10:38,704 --> 00:10:40,664
When, when may the
ambassador leave?

102
00:10:42,375 --> 00:10:44,286
State Department's
confirmed his identity.

103
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:45,310
You can go now.

104
00:10:46,713 --> 00:10:47,890
Wait, waits, wait. Sir.

105
00:10:47,914 --> 00:10:50,314
They think I'm
guilty. Sir. Please.

106
00:11:00,226 --> 00:11:01,325
Oh, hey!

107
00:11:01,394 --> 00:11:02,471
There's an egg roll left.

108
00:11:02,495 --> 00:11:03,973
Oh, I couldn't eat another bite.

109
00:11:03,997 --> 00:11:05,441
I'm just absolutely stuffed.

110
00:11:05,465 --> 00:11:06,898
Go ahead, kill it.

111
00:11:06,966 --> 00:11:08,065
Oh, all right.

112
00:11:09,902 --> 00:11:11,380
You know, for a skinny fella,

113
00:11:11,404 --> 00:11:12,870
you can really pack it away.

114
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:16,241
Well...

115
00:11:17,577 --> 00:11:19,444
I'll get this.

116
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,280
Hello?

117
00:11:23,350 --> 00:11:25,450
Oh, hello, Alex.

118
00:11:27,019 --> 00:11:29,287
No, kid... no kidding!

119
00:11:30,357 --> 00:11:32,624
Benson, Reese, Kline & Dunhill?

120
00:11:32,692 --> 00:11:34,492
Oh, that's big-time stuff.

121
00:11:34,561 --> 00:11:38,029
I always thought you
would end up in New York.

122
00:11:38,098 --> 00:11:40,031
Yeah.

123
00:11:41,267 --> 00:11:44,135
Oh, well... That all depends.

124
00:11:44,204 --> 00:11:46,571
Some-Sometimes
higher, sometimes lower.

125
00:11:46,639 --> 00:11:49,140
I usually charge whatever
the traffic will allow.

126
00:11:49,209 --> 00:11:51,120
And this guy doesn't
sound like he's hurting.

127
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:56,280
Uh-huh. Oh!

128
00:11:56,349 --> 00:11:58,983
Yeah, well,
let's... Yeah, well...

129
00:11:59,052 --> 00:12:00,763
Well, we'll have
to think about that.

130
00:12:00,787 --> 00:12:03,354
Now, listen. Listen, if
you talk to the family,

131
00:12:03,423 --> 00:12:05,423
say hello to your mother.

132
00:12:05,491 --> 00:12:07,425
Okay.

133
00:12:08,929 --> 00:12:10,073
Going to New York?

134
00:12:10,097 --> 00:12:11,763
Well, I might.

135
00:12:11,832 --> 00:12:13,331
Hit the "in" places.

136
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:14,799
The "in" places?

137
00:12:14,868 --> 00:12:16,634
Restaurants, Benj.

138
00:12:16,703 --> 00:12:19,304
You know, where you
slip the doorman four bits,

139
00:12:19,372 --> 00:12:21,472
and get the best
table in the house.

140
00:12:22,975 --> 00:12:24,509
50 cents?

141
00:12:24,578 --> 00:12:25,810
Well, it's the big time.

142
00:12:25,879 --> 00:12:28,079
You got to spread
it around a little.

143
00:12:28,148 --> 00:12:29,668
Yeah.

144
00:12:29,715 --> 00:12:32,617
Oh. Here's some
shrimp chow mein.

145
00:12:32,685 --> 00:12:34,052
Oh, no. Please, please.

146
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:35,486
Don't even suggest it.

147
00:12:35,555 --> 00:12:37,488
Looks good. Still warm.

148
00:12:37,557 --> 00:12:40,558
I... Uh... Oh, all right.

149
00:12:46,566 --> 00:12:48,144
<i>Mr. Stewart is now home?</i>

150
00:12:48,168 --> 00:12:49,478
<i>That's correct.</i>

151
00:12:49,502 --> 00:12:50,980
<i>That's excellent.</i>

152
00:12:51,004 --> 00:12:54,038
Well, you've done
yeoman-like work on this, Alex.

153
00:12:54,107 --> 00:12:56,307
Now, London would like
to close the book on this

154
00:12:56,376 --> 00:12:58,709
as quickly as possible,
so when can we expect

155
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:00,322
Mr. Stewart to
leave the country?

156
00:13:00,346 --> 00:13:01,891
Uh, well, there's...

157
00:13:01,915 --> 00:13:04,260
There's a... a small problem.

158
00:13:04,284 --> 00:13:05,617
Problem, Alex?

159
00:13:05,685 --> 00:13:07,518
Yes, sir.

160
00:13:07,587 --> 00:13:09,187
Mr. Stewart believes

161
00:13:09,255 --> 00:13:11,135
that invoking
diplomatic immunity

162
00:13:11,191 --> 00:13:14,592
is-is tantamount to
a-a confession of guilt.

163
00:13:17,163 --> 00:13:19,597
Just what does
Mr. Stewart plan to do?

164
00:13:19,665 --> 00:13:22,132
Well, sir, uh,
Mr. Stewart is determined

165
00:13:22,201 --> 00:13:23,601
to defend himself.

166
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:26,270
Well, we can't
get involved in this.

167
00:13:26,338 --> 00:13:27,471
Well, now,

168
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:29,418
we-we could find
him a criminal lawyer.

169
00:13:29,442 --> 00:13:30,953
Well, I was thinking...

170
00:13:30,977 --> 00:13:33,310
If I... if I could
make a suggestion...

171
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:35,913
That we-we find
him a criminal lawyer.

172
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:37,648
A... A murder one specialist.

173
00:13:37,717 --> 00:13:42,052
Uh, say, like
maybe... Ben Matlock.

174
00:13:45,157 --> 00:13:46,991
It's just an idea.

175
00:13:47,059 --> 00:13:49,694
I think I've heard
of Benjamin Matlock

176
00:13:49,763 --> 00:13:51,028
from... from Atlanta?

177
00:13:51,097 --> 00:13:52,241
That's right.

178
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:53,498
He's supposed to be good.

179
00:13:53,566 --> 00:13:54,665
Supposed to be good?

180
00:13:54,734 --> 00:13:55,900
He's the best.

181
00:13:55,969 --> 00:13:58,002
Yeah, I thought
maybe he could come in

182
00:13:58,071 --> 00:14:00,015
and do a preliminary
investigation, you know,

183
00:14:00,039 --> 00:14:01,851
and if-if he could
find enough evidence

184
00:14:01,875 --> 00:14:04,441
to prove that Mr. Stewart
is innocent to a jury,

185
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:07,245
then, well, we... we let him.

186
00:14:07,313 --> 00:14:11,115
So, we make Anthony Stewart
Ben Matlock's problem, not ours?

187
00:14:11,184 --> 00:14:13,028
We distance
ourselves from the case,

188
00:14:13,052 --> 00:14:17,188
so that if it doesn't
work... I like it.

189
00:14:19,359 --> 00:14:20,658
You'll arrange it?

190
00:14:20,727 --> 00:14:22,627
Yes, sir. I'll sure try.

191
00:14:22,696 --> 00:14:24,329
Good.

192
00:14:24,397 --> 00:14:26,330
Gentlemen.

193
00:15:21,554 --> 00:15:24,488
♪ ♪

194
00:15:24,557 --> 00:15:25,837
Excuse me.

195
00:15:30,129 --> 00:15:32,107
Well, there you are.

196
00:15:32,131 --> 00:15:33,464
Yeah.

197
00:15:33,533 --> 00:15:35,967
Oh! Hot dog!

198
00:15:36,036 --> 00:15:37,468
How many?

199
00:15:37,537 --> 00:15:38,781
One!

200
00:15:38,805 --> 00:15:40,704
All beef?

201
00:15:41,808 --> 00:15:43,141
Ketchup?

202
00:15:43,209 --> 00:15:45,008
Yeah. And mustard.

203
00:15:48,114 --> 00:15:49,213
Buck 75.

204
00:15:49,282 --> 00:15:51,048
Buck 75?

205
00:15:51,117 --> 00:15:53,151
Buck 75.

206
00:15:53,219 --> 00:15:54,499
For one hot dog?

207
00:15:54,554 --> 00:15:55,753
Buck 75.

208
00:15:55,822 --> 00:15:57,755
At home, I get a hot dog

209
00:15:57,823 --> 00:16:00,057
with mustard and
ketchup for 75 cents.

210
00:16:00,126 --> 00:16:03,494
And onions and chili and cheese

211
00:16:03,563 --> 00:16:05,163
and relish and a pickle.

212
00:16:05,231 --> 00:16:07,331
That'll cost you
two and a quarter.

213
00:16:07,399 --> 00:16:08,999
Well, hell fire!

214
00:16:09,068 --> 00:16:11,635
If hot dogs are going
for that much up here,

215
00:16:11,704 --> 00:16:14,004
I'll buy the whole damn stand!

216
00:16:18,845 --> 00:16:20,778
My wallet's gone.

217
00:16:20,847 --> 00:16:22,913
Somebody stole my...

218
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:25,849
That guy in the plaid
coat picked my wallet!

219
00:16:25,919 --> 00:16:27,629
Hey, that... That guy...

220
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:29,098
That guy took my wallet!

221
00:16:29,122 --> 00:16:30,421
That...

222
00:16:30,489 --> 00:16:32,768
He just took...
just took my wallet!

223
00:16:32,792 --> 00:16:34,725
Yeah.

224
00:16:46,406 --> 00:16:48,105
I got a dollar 14.

225
00:16:48,174 --> 00:16:50,908
Buck 75.

226
00:16:50,977 --> 00:16:52,243
Ben! Hi.

227
00:16:52,312 --> 00:16:53,410
Hi!

228
00:16:53,479 --> 00:16:55,390
Oh, hi.

229
00:16:55,414 --> 00:16:56,981
Oh, hi, Alex!

230
00:16:57,050 --> 00:16:58,282
Hi, Ben. How are you?

231
00:16:58,351 --> 00:16:59,628
Hey, good to see you.

232
00:16:59,652 --> 00:17:00,930
Good to see you. Wow!

233
00:17:00,954 --> 00:17:02,831
My mom says hi. Oh, good.

234
00:17:02,855 --> 00:17:04,666
Yeah. Thank you.
Oh, you've grown up.

235
00:17:04,690 --> 00:17:05,790
Yeah, I've grown out.

236
00:17:05,858 --> 00:17:07,302
Oh. I-I got a little...

237
00:17:07,326 --> 00:17:09,004
What-What's it been,
ten years, something?

238
00:17:09,028 --> 00:17:10,239
Ten years, yeah. Oh, wow.

239
00:17:10,263 --> 00:17:11,695
I'm so glad that you came.

240
00:17:11,764 --> 00:17:14,476
I want to give you something
before we go any further. Oh.

241
00:17:14,500 --> 00:17:16,245
There you go. Oh.

242
00:17:16,269 --> 00:17:18,413
"Benson, Reese, Cline, Dunhill.

243
00:17:18,437 --> 00:17:20,615
Alex Winthrop, Attorney at Law."

244
00:17:20,639 --> 00:17:23,307
Oh, actually, I'm just a
numbers cruncher. I'm...

245
00:17:23,375 --> 00:17:25,220
What's that? I
work in the office.

246
00:17:25,244 --> 00:17:27,156
I do taxes and books and... Oh.

247
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:30,214
I'm just so excited that you
came and took this case,

248
00:17:30,282 --> 00:17:32,984
and I've always enjoyed
telling people that I know you,

249
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:35,653
and now they've assigned me
to you, so if you need any help,

250
00:17:35,721 --> 00:17:37,721
if I can get you anything,
if you need anything,

251
00:17:37,790 --> 00:17:39,023
just tell me, anything.

252
00:17:39,092 --> 00:17:41,692
I need 61 cents.

253
00:17:41,761 --> 00:17:44,095
61 cents?

254
00:17:44,163 --> 00:17:46,630
For my hot dog, Alex.

255
00:18:01,214 --> 00:18:02,357
<i>Well,</i>

256
00:18:02,381 --> 00:18:05,749
I think we can say
one thing for certain.

257
00:18:05,818 --> 00:18:08,986
You have a definite
problem with women.

258
00:18:09,055 --> 00:18:11,015
Having an affair
with Shelby Russell

259
00:18:11,057 --> 00:18:14,624
was not exactly an exercise
in good judgment, I admit.

260
00:18:14,693 --> 00:18:16,404
Throwing her husband head first

261
00:18:16,428 --> 00:18:18,941
into the hors d'oeuvres
wasn't real smart either.

262
00:18:18,965 --> 00:18:20,430
Well, I didn't throw him.

263
00:18:20,499 --> 00:18:21,732
He fell.

264
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:23,300
More or less.

265
00:18:23,369 --> 00:18:25,535
He wouldn't give
me my watch back.

266
00:18:25,604 --> 00:18:28,505
The Prime Minister
herself gave me that watch.

267
00:18:28,574 --> 00:18:31,720
You must've been
desperate to get it back.

268
00:18:31,744 --> 00:18:33,443
Not that desperate, Mr. Matlock.

269
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:35,624
Ambassador,
picture, please, sir!

270
00:18:35,648 --> 00:18:36,692
Sir, look this way, please.

271
00:18:36,716 --> 00:18:38,396
Gentlemen, can you
please move away?!

272
00:18:39,251 --> 00:18:41,852
Go straight to your
apartment; stay there.

273
00:18:41,921 --> 00:18:44,299
Don't talk to the press,
don't answer the phone.

274
00:18:44,323 --> 00:18:46,524
Don't mess with anybody's wife.

275
00:18:46,592 --> 00:18:47,703
Ambassador Stewart!

276
00:18:47,727 --> 00:18:49,359
Ambassador Stewart, please!

277
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:04,308
Lieutenant Rosetti?
Alexander Winter.

278
00:19:04,377 --> 00:19:05,855
Yes, I remember who you are you.

279
00:19:05,879 --> 00:19:07,812
Uh, this is Benjamin Matlock.

280
00:19:07,881 --> 00:19:09,157
He's representing
Anthony Stewart.

281
00:19:09,181 --> 00:19:10,793
Nice to meet you,
Lieutenant. How are you?

282
00:19:10,817 --> 00:19:11,749
I was just mugged.

283
00:19:11,818 --> 00:19:13,150
No kidding.

284
00:19:13,219 --> 00:19:15,252
Yeah, uh, two
blocks from my hotel.

285
00:19:15,321 --> 00:19:17,287
Took my cash, my credit cards,

286
00:19:17,356 --> 00:19:19,301
everything; took my wallet
right out of my pocket.

287
00:19:19,325 --> 00:19:20,402
That right? Yeah.

288
00:19:20,426 --> 00:19:22,093
I got a good look
at him, though.

289
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:25,129
Had on a plaid coat, appeared
to be, oh... in his mid-30s.

290
00:19:25,198 --> 00:19:26,842
Why don't you go
down to the precinct

291
00:19:26,866 --> 00:19:28,766
and file a complaint, hmm?

292
00:19:30,670 --> 00:19:33,404
Well, I thought
while you were here...

293
00:19:33,473 --> 00:19:35,105
Complaints are not my job.

294
00:19:37,443 --> 00:19:39,910
You think this candlestick is
a mate to the murder weapon?

295
00:19:39,979 --> 00:19:42,179
Who knows.

296
00:19:42,248 --> 00:19:44,348
Lieutenant Rosetti,
we'd like to examine

297
00:19:44,417 --> 00:19:46,395
the murder weapon,
if you don't mind.

298
00:19:46,419 --> 00:19:48,464
Well, according to Mrs. Russell,

299
00:19:48,488 --> 00:19:50,999
Anthony Stewart took it
with him and dumped it.

300
00:19:51,023 --> 00:19:55,225
Uh, Lieutenant, a
man killed somebody,

301
00:19:55,294 --> 00:19:56,861
in front of an eyewitness,

302
00:19:56,929 --> 00:20:00,030
yet goes to the trouble of
disposing of the murder weapon.

303
00:20:00,099 --> 00:20:01,377
Makes you wonder
whatever happened

304
00:20:01,401 --> 00:20:02,933
to common sense, doesn't it?

305
00:20:03,002 --> 00:20:05,247
What can I tell
you? Come on, guys,

306
00:20:05,271 --> 00:20:06,381
we're out of here.

307
00:20:06,405 --> 00:20:07,565
Hmm...

308
00:20:11,377 --> 00:20:12,855
What is this...? I don't know.

309
00:20:15,681 --> 00:20:17,959
You talk to this
Shelby Russell yet?

310
00:20:17,983 --> 00:20:19,249
No, I haven't.

311
00:20:19,318 --> 00:20:21,852
She gave her statement to the
D.A. and she just disappeared.

312
00:20:21,921 --> 00:20:23,387
Hmm.

313
00:20:23,456 --> 00:20:25,033
A lot of D.A.'s like to play

314
00:20:25,057 --> 00:20:27,436
hide and seek with
their star witnesses.

315
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:30,027
How did Anthony and
Mrs. Russell get together?

316
00:20:30,095 --> 00:20:32,029
Michael Russell owned a
pharmaceutical company.

317
00:20:32,097 --> 00:20:34,565
They did a lot of business
with third world countries.

318
00:20:34,634 --> 00:20:36,500
Anthony met him
and his wife, Shelby,

319
00:20:36,569 --> 00:20:37,946
at some embassy function. Hmm.

320
00:20:37,970 --> 00:20:40,938
You know, I-I've
heard that women just

321
00:20:41,006 --> 00:20:43,373
throw themselves
at the Ambassador.

322
00:20:43,443 --> 00:20:44,909
Oh, yeah?

323
00:20:44,977 --> 00:20:46,677
Must be the accent.

324
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:50,626
Does this pharmaceutical
company have an address?

325
00:20:50,650 --> 00:20:51,982
Yeah.

326
00:21:02,261 --> 00:21:03,560
Mm.

327
00:21:03,629 --> 00:21:04,928
Hmm.

328
00:21:14,139 --> 00:21:15,606
Where'd you get those keys?

329
00:21:15,674 --> 00:21:16,954
Back at the penthouse.

330
00:21:17,009 --> 00:21:18,442
They were in a desk drawer.

331
00:21:39,465 --> 00:21:40,764
Hi.

332
00:21:41,867 --> 00:21:43,387
Hold it!

333
00:21:50,209 --> 00:21:51,541
Hey, wait a minute!

334
00:21:58,817 --> 00:21:59,916
Ben!

335
00:22:08,794 --> 00:22:11,540
Fella, get out of the street!

336
00:22:12,798 --> 00:22:13,898
What are you doing?!

337
00:22:17,403 --> 00:22:18,502
Hey!

338
00:22:22,341 --> 00:22:24,085
I know who that guy is!

339
00:22:24,109 --> 00:22:25,208
Who?

340
00:22:25,278 --> 00:22:26,722
That's Robert
Unger; he's a lawyer.

341
00:22:26,746 --> 00:22:28,245
He works for Thomas Baldwin.

342
00:22:28,314 --> 00:22:29,758
We did a business deal for them.

343
00:22:29,782 --> 00:22:30,782
Who's Thomas Baldwin?

344
00:22:30,849 --> 00:22:32,249
He's a real estate developer.

345
00:22:32,318 --> 00:22:35,552
He's worth millions; a
very prominent citizen.

346
00:22:35,621 --> 00:22:36,621
Yeah?

347
00:22:43,696 --> 00:22:45,741
<i>What's the deal here? There's no</i>

348
00:22:45,765 --> 00:22:47,309
secretaries, no filing cabinets,

349
00:22:47,333 --> 00:22:48,910
no coffee machines, no copiers,

350
00:22:48,934 --> 00:22:52,402
no personalized pens.

351
00:22:52,471 --> 00:22:55,872
Well, big mail drop.

352
00:22:55,941 --> 00:22:56,873
Right. Yeah.

353
00:22:56,942 --> 00:22:58,222
Right.

354
00:22:59,345 --> 00:23:01,845
Uh, try one of those keys here.

355
00:23:12,858 --> 00:23:15,292
Oh...

356
00:23:19,498 --> 00:23:22,967
Somebody paid
somebody a lot of money!

357
00:23:23,035 --> 00:23:24,035
Yeah.

358
00:23:25,671 --> 00:23:28,204
No indication where it came from

359
00:23:28,274 --> 00:23:31,275
or who it went to.

360
00:23:31,343 --> 00:23:34,811
Just... A, B and C.

361
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:38,883
What do you want to bet that
one of them is Thomas Baldwin?

362
00:23:38,951 --> 00:23:41,118
Huh.

363
00:23:43,055 --> 00:23:44,366
<i>They argued...</i>

364
00:23:44,390 --> 00:23:47,391
and Michael hit him in the face.

365
00:23:47,460 --> 00:23:49,626
Hard.

366
00:23:49,695 --> 00:23:54,698
And Anthony, well,
he just went berserk.

367
00:23:54,767 --> 00:23:58,602
So, he grabbed a
candlestick from the mantle.

368
00:23:58,671 --> 00:24:02,506
And he hit Michael in the head.

369
00:24:02,575 --> 00:24:04,408
Once, maybe twice, I don't know.

370
00:24:04,477 --> 00:24:06,276
I... I closed my eyes.

371
00:24:06,345 --> 00:24:08,978
It was horrible!

372
00:24:09,048 --> 00:24:10,981
Take your time, Mrs. Russell.

373
00:24:13,753 --> 00:24:15,619
When I opened them,

374
00:24:15,688 --> 00:24:17,955
he'd stopped
and-and he was just...

375
00:24:18,024 --> 00:24:20,090
just standing
there looking at me.

376
00:24:20,159 --> 00:24:22,593
So, I ran into the bedroom
and I locked the door.

377
00:24:22,662 --> 00:24:24,060
And I called the police.

378
00:24:25,664 --> 00:24:27,509
And I-I didn't open
it until they got there

379
00:24:27,533 --> 00:24:30,467
and by that time he'd gone.

380
00:24:30,536 --> 00:24:34,004
I know this wasn't easy
for you, Mrs. Russell.

381
00:24:34,073 --> 00:24:36,707
Thank you.

382
00:24:36,776 --> 00:24:40,055
Mr. Matlock, do you have
any questions for this witness?

383
00:24:40,079 --> 00:24:42,246
You betcha!

384
00:24:42,314 --> 00:24:43,813
That is, with your permission.

385
00:24:43,882 --> 00:24:44,949
Go ahead.

386
00:24:47,553 --> 00:24:48,986
Hello.

387
00:24:49,054 --> 00:24:52,723
Mrs. Russell, this is the first
time I've actually seen you,

388
00:24:52,792 --> 00:24:55,425
but I can certainly understand

389
00:24:55,495 --> 00:24:58,529
why Mr. Stewart was
so attracted to you.

390
00:24:58,598 --> 00:25:00,730
Thank you.

391
00:25:00,799 --> 00:25:04,935
You're a very difficult person
to get ahold of, Mrs. Russell.

392
00:25:05,004 --> 00:25:07,938
Well, I didn't want him
to know where I was.

393
00:25:08,007 --> 00:25:09,952
He'd already gotten
away with one murder,

394
00:25:09,976 --> 00:25:12,020
and I was afraid
he'd try to make it two.

395
00:25:12,044 --> 00:25:13,143
Move to strike!

396
00:25:13,212 --> 00:25:14,723
You opened the door, Mr.Matlock.

397
00:25:14,747 --> 00:25:17,114
She's entitled to
respond to your comment.

398
00:25:18,217 --> 00:25:19,583
Um...

399
00:25:19,652 --> 00:25:22,919
How long were you and,
um, Mr. Russell married?

400
00:25:22,989 --> 00:25:24,922
We married a year
ago on the Cape.

401
00:25:24,990 --> 00:25:26,256
Nantucket.

402
00:25:28,127 --> 00:25:30,227
And how much did you know

403
00:25:30,296 --> 00:25:32,162
about the pharmaceutical company

404
00:25:32,231 --> 00:25:34,031
- that he owned?
- Objection!

405
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:35,999
This is a preliminary hearing.

406
00:25:36,068 --> 00:25:37,867
The only issue here
is whether or not

407
00:25:37,936 --> 00:25:39,236
a crime has been committed.

408
00:25:39,305 --> 00:25:41,082
Or whether or not
Anthony Stewart should be

409
00:25:41,106 --> 00:25:42,339
bound over for trial.

410
00:25:42,408 --> 00:25:43,718
My question goes to motive.

411
00:25:43,742 --> 00:25:45,520
I believe that's been...
I'm trying to show

412
00:25:45,544 --> 00:25:47,088
that someone else
could've had a motive.

413
00:25:47,112 --> 00:25:48,356
You're fishing! You're fishing!

414
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:50,158
I have a right to pursue
motive! Objection!

415
00:25:50,182 --> 00:25:51,359
Mr. Matlock! Objection!

416
00:25:51,383 --> 00:25:52,694
I'm just trying to
question this witness

417
00:25:52,718 --> 00:25:54,162
before she once again
disappears into the unknown...

418
00:25:54,186 --> 00:25:55,763
- Your Honor?! Objection!
- Mr. Matlock!

419
00:25:55,787 --> 00:25:58,634
Perhaps never to be seen
again! Mr. Matlock! Mr. Matlock!

420
00:25:58,658 --> 00:26:00,591
I am sustaining the objection.

421
00:26:00,659 --> 00:26:02,726
That's impossible.

422
00:26:02,795 --> 00:26:04,094
Mr. Matlock, I'm warning you.

423
00:26:04,163 --> 00:26:06,463
I have a right to
ask the question.

424
00:26:06,532 --> 00:26:08,999
I am sustaining the objection.

425
00:26:09,067 --> 00:26:11,602
You may step down.

426
00:26:11,670 --> 00:26:14,137
I have a right to
ask the question!

427
00:26:14,206 --> 00:26:15,572
Objection!

428
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:16,974
I will cite you for contempt!

429
00:26:21,580 --> 00:26:26,683
The court finds reason to
believe that the crime of murder

430
00:26:26,752 --> 00:26:29,252
in the first degree
has been committed.

431
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:34,391
Considering the special
circumstances in this case,

432
00:26:34,460 --> 00:26:38,896
the defendant, Anthony Stewart,
shall be bound over for trial

433
00:26:38,964 --> 00:26:42,932
in this court on
October 19 at 10:00 a.m.

434
00:26:43,002 --> 00:26:44,479
Court is adjourned.

435
00:26:44,503 --> 00:26:45,614
Shelby!

436
00:26:47,673 --> 00:26:49,373
Shelby!

437
00:26:49,441 --> 00:26:51,408
Ambassador! Ambassador!

438
00:27:03,856 --> 00:27:06,022
Please, please.

439
00:27:06,091 --> 00:27:07,824
No comment!

440
00:27:07,893 --> 00:27:10,194
Okay, come on, fellas!

441
00:27:10,262 --> 00:27:13,463
That's enough! That's
enough! Give the lady a break.

442
00:27:17,969 --> 00:27:19,781
Come on, guys,
she gave you plenty.

443
00:27:19,805 --> 00:27:21,371
Huh? Get on out of here!

444
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:23,139
Just give us a statement here!

445
00:27:23,208 --> 00:27:24,419
Are you all right?

446
00:27:24,443 --> 00:27:26,543
Yes, I didn't know
what I was going to do.

447
00:27:26,612 --> 00:27:28,223
Whoever you are,
thank you very much.

448
00:27:28,247 --> 00:27:29,324
That's no problem.

449
00:27:29,348 --> 00:27:30,425
Need a cab?

450
00:27:30,449 --> 00:27:31,848
Yes, please.

451
00:27:33,919 --> 00:27:35,318
Taxi, come on, come on.

452
00:27:35,388 --> 00:27:36,999
Come on, guys, leave her alone.

453
00:27:37,023 --> 00:27:38,489
Where are you going?

454
00:27:38,557 --> 00:27:40,424
Gramercy Park Hotel.

455
00:27:40,493 --> 00:27:42,993
Come on, back up,
guys, give her a break.

456
00:27:43,062 --> 00:27:45,128
Gramercy Park... step on it.

457
00:27:45,197 --> 00:27:47,530
Come on, guys, back it up.

458
00:27:47,599 --> 00:27:48,865
Geez.

459
00:27:48,934 --> 00:27:52,869
Aw, go on, get out of here.

460
00:27:55,874 --> 00:27:57,941
She's at the
Gramercy Park Hotel.

461
00:27:58,010 --> 00:27:59,290
You got it.

462
00:28:06,485 --> 00:28:07,417
Good morning, Helen.

463
00:28:07,486 --> 00:28:09,197
Good morning, Mr. Baldwin.

464
00:28:09,221 --> 00:28:10,753
Ben Matlock is waiting

465
00:28:10,823 --> 00:28:13,090
inside for you. He said
you were expecting him?

466
00:28:15,193 --> 00:28:16,426
Thank you.

467
00:28:18,831 --> 00:28:22,265
No, no, my name is not Murdock.

468
00:28:22,334 --> 00:28:23,266
It's Matlock, Matlock.

469
00:28:23,335 --> 00:28:26,269
I came in town yesterday.

470
00:28:26,338 --> 00:28:28,372
Never mind, I'll call you back.

471
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:30,874
Somebody stole my wallet.

472
00:28:30,942 --> 00:28:33,009
Had to cancel my credit cards,

473
00:28:33,079 --> 00:28:35,579
send for a new driver's license,
have my bank wire me money,

474
00:28:35,647 --> 00:28:37,047
and the police are still trying

475
00:28:37,116 --> 00:28:39,561
to figure out how to spell
my name. I hope you have

476
00:28:39,585 --> 00:28:41,818
a very good reason for
being in here, Mister...

477
00:28:41,887 --> 00:28:42,819
Matlock, Matlock.

478
00:28:42,888 --> 00:28:44,721
Ben Matlock... I'm an attorney.

479
00:28:44,790 --> 00:28:47,124
Uh, I represent Anthony Stewart.

480
00:28:47,193 --> 00:28:49,759
Uh, your associate...

481
00:28:49,828 --> 00:28:52,396
Your secretary... was kind
enough to tell me his name is

482
00:28:52,465 --> 00:28:53,396
Robert Unger.

483
00:28:53,466 --> 00:28:56,032
I assume he told
you that we kind of

484
00:28:56,101 --> 00:28:58,702
ran into each other yesterday
at Michael Russell's offices.

485
00:28:58,771 --> 00:29:00,837
He didn't?

486
00:29:00,906 --> 00:29:02,038
Oh, Michael Russell is

487
00:29:02,108 --> 00:29:04,741
the man that my client is
supposed to have murdered,

488
00:29:04,810 --> 00:29:08,011
and he rented office spaces
at 470 Madison Avenue.

489
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:10,013
That's one of your
buildings, isn't it?

490
00:29:10,081 --> 00:29:11,459
It's one of my buildings, yes,

491
00:29:11,483 --> 00:29:14,051
but I don't involve
myself in rentals.

492
00:29:14,119 --> 00:29:15,652
I have people who do that.

493
00:29:15,720 --> 00:29:17,165
Uh, like Robert Unger.

494
00:29:17,189 --> 00:29:18,466
To tell you the truth,

495
00:29:18,490 --> 00:29:21,425
it looked to me like he
was ransacking the place.

496
00:29:21,493 --> 00:29:23,360
Uh, Robert supervises the people

497
00:29:23,429 --> 00:29:24,962
who manage my buildings.

498
00:29:25,030 --> 00:29:26,896
I'm sure he was...
I don't know...

499
00:29:26,966 --> 00:29:28,298
Looking for a key?

500
00:29:29,401 --> 00:29:31,713
Ask him and let
me know, will you?

501
00:29:31,737 --> 00:29:36,039
I wonder if he
was looking for this.

502
00:29:36,108 --> 00:29:37,040
What is that?

503
00:29:37,109 --> 00:29:38,508
It's a ledger.

504
00:29:38,577 --> 00:29:40,454
It's a ledger for
Michael Russell's

505
00:29:40,478 --> 00:29:42,089
pharmaceutical company,

506
00:29:42,113 --> 00:29:44,914
and there's something very
odd about that company.

507
00:29:44,984 --> 00:29:47,884
All that office space
he rented from you...

508
00:29:47,953 --> 00:29:51,721
Empty, like it's one big front.

509
00:29:51,790 --> 00:29:54,069
And, and all these
profits disbursed

510
00:29:54,093 --> 00:29:58,528
to companies or persons
only listed as "A," "B," and "C."

511
00:29:58,597 --> 00:30:00,709
What would be interesting

512
00:30:00,733 --> 00:30:04,567
is to find if somebody
has bank deposits

513
00:30:04,636 --> 00:30:07,270
that correspond
to these figures.

514
00:30:07,339 --> 00:30:09,940
I know what.

515
00:30:10,008 --> 00:30:14,277
I could leave the ledger with
you, you show it to Robert,

516
00:30:14,346 --> 00:30:16,191
see what he knows about it,

517
00:30:16,215 --> 00:30:17,648
then you get it back to me.

518
00:30:17,716 --> 00:30:19,694
Yeah, that would...
no, I'd better keep it.

519
00:30:19,718 --> 00:30:21,051
I know, uh,

520
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:24,454
you're busy and I'll
get out of your way.

521
00:30:24,523 --> 00:30:27,824
I hope I didn't
come at a bad time.

522
00:30:27,892 --> 00:30:29,959
That's quite all right.

523
00:30:40,539 --> 00:30:42,472
Something has gone very wrong.

524
00:30:42,541 --> 00:30:45,842
Oh, and, and thank you.

525
00:30:47,045 --> 00:30:48,712
For the use of the phone.

526
00:30:48,780 --> 00:30:49,713
Oh.

527
00:30:49,781 --> 00:30:51,126
So I, I guess I'll, uh,

528
00:30:51,150 --> 00:30:52,982
I'll see you at the trial.

529
00:30:53,052 --> 00:30:56,986
I doubt that I'd have anything
to contribute, Mr. Matlock.

530
00:30:57,056 --> 00:30:58,199
Well, you never know.

531
00:30:58,223 --> 00:30:59,523
Things change.

532
00:30:59,591 --> 00:31:03,359
And, uh, thank
you again for the...

533
00:31:07,966 --> 00:31:10,566
We have to meet tonight.

534
00:31:16,375 --> 00:31:17,585
